        "content": "{\"answer_text\":\"A bare report of an unauthorised transaction is not, by itself, a complaint. RG 271.33(d) says ASIC does not consider reports of unauthorised transactions under the ePayments Code to be complaints. But it is a complaint if the consumer raises separate issues related to the transaction that meet the definition of a complaint, or expresses dissatisfaction with the outcome or handling of the unauthorised transaction. The relevant definition elements are that there is an expression of dissatisfaction made to or about the organisation, related to its products, services, staff or the handling of a complaint, where a response or resolution is explicitly or implicitly expected or legally required. ASIC explains that a response is explicitly expected if the consumer clearly requests it, and implicitly expected if the consumer raises the dissatisfaction in a way that implies they reasonably expect the firm to respond and/or take specific action.\",\"citations\":[\"asic_rg271-p0015-c001-29fdc2ba5f4c\",\"asic_rg271-p0013-c001-a53d7d32bf80\",\"asic_rg271-p0014-c001-2450831dba3b\"],\"confidence\":0.98,\"output_type\":\"ANSWER\"}",
